al mental  performance enhancers Get Smart drink  neuro they suggest it's pretty simple to  test claims of cognitive enhancement in  a well-designed study double-blind  placebo-controlled but the company has  yet to publish such an experiment well  it's adding caffeine to a drink in a  pretty bottle can make it fly off the  shelves why bother with the study it's  hard to make money off of readily  available food you can't patent  blueberries and that means that  expensive clinical trials on their  efficacy are hard to fund which means  that we don't have a regulatory body  like the FDA helping us separate the  wheat from the chaff it's hard to find  data on the effects of different  specific foods on brain function hard  but not impossible  one way you might say is just to ask  people to participate in studies that  test their brain function and track what  they eat simple enough until you  consider that the types of people who  can afford to volunteer for such studies  remember there probably isn't much money  to pay them so most survey studies are  limited to people with a moderate to  high socioeconomic status the second  problem is that you need to control the  dosage or ingredients in whatever it is  that you're studying so for example  let's say you're interested in studying  the effects of resveratrol which is an  antioxidant found in red wine well for  such a study you need to know exactly  how much red wine is being consumed you  can't just ask people to report how many  glasses they drink the size of my glass  might be very different from the size of  yours the gold standard a double-blind  placebo-controlled study is expensive  but ultimately that's the own  we kind of evidence that can really  evaluate the effect of a particular food  on brain function so as we talk about  the results of specific studies let's  keep these constraints in mind  and speaking of minds what do we know  about how nutrients reach the brain  well you might remember that the way  nutrients reach brain cells is a bit  different from the rest of the body  that's because brain cells are precious  we don't want to lose them because they  don't replenish the way say our skin  cells do our experiences are written  into the physical and physiological  features of our neurons and we don't  want to lose our memory of our  grandmother for example because we ate a  bad oyster which obviously is impossible  but you get the point the immune system  largely functions by killing cells  infected with foreign invaders well we  can't use that system in the brain the  same way that we use it in the rest of  the body so instead of sending out  troops of killer cells Nature built a  fortress around the brain called the  blood-brain barrier it's essentially  just a network of blood vessels with  cells that are much more tightly packed  preventing large molecules from being  able to cross into the brain but that  means that important nutrients are also  shut out so the brain has a complex  system of cells that do security checks  letting in the good nutrients and  passing them along to the right brain  cells and keeping out the suicide  bombers for the most part you might also  remember that the brain is hungry it's a  metabolically costly organ taking up  only 2% of body mass but guzzling around  20% of its fuel that's why it's hard to  think when your blood sugar is low but  providing glucose is not the only way  that food can influence the brain it  also provides certain cellular building  materials that can play a role in  neuroplasticity how cells change with  experience these changes can even  include epigenetics switching genes on  and off and affecting how they're  expressed meaning that what you eat can  ultimately shape your brain let me show  you how  foods for example can trigger the  release of certain hormones that can  then affect brain function one of these  hormones is called BDNF brain-derived  neurotrophic factor and it plays a  starring role in our first food study  BDNF is one of my favorite proteins so  let me take a minute to tell you why it  has a number of jobs one of which is to  guide and promote the growth of new  neurons and the connections between them  it's very active in my favorite brain  region the hippocampus which is where  our short-term memories are turned into  long-term ones the hippocampus is our  window both to the past and to the  future we need it to travel backward in  time in our memory and forward in time  in our imagination pretty awesome  because it plays a big role in shaping  the hippocampus BDNF is important for  long-term memory formation if you put an  animal human or otherwise  in an enriching environment for example  more B D and s will be produced in his  brain leading to more synapses more  dendrites and more neurons themself the  ultimate effect of which is that the  animal learns more it gets smarter than  an animal raised in an impoverished  environment whose brain has lower levels  of BDNF and fewer of these changes so is  there any connection between B D and s  levels and the food we eat well  the key ingredient in fish oil is  omega-3 polyunsaturated acid  specifically docos EXO Noack acid or DHA  and you might have heard claims that is  good for your brain you can find it an  oily fish like salmon mackerel and  sardines or you can take it as a  supplement and yes DHA is thought to  play a role in brain development by  increasing expression of BDNF this is  why women who are pregnant are  encouraged to take DHA and you can find  infant formulas with DHA supplements  added in from work with rodents we know  that omega-3 fatty acids turn on genes  that help keep the signaling system  between brain cells working properly  and enable neuroplasticity or the types  of physical changes that drive learning  DHA is the most prevalent fatty acid in  brain cell membranes the protective  covering that keeps the good stuff  inside the cell and the bad stuff out  but that also plays a major role in  signaling between cells and there's some  evidence that our ability to add DHA to  our diets with a turning point in our  evolutionary history helping our brains  to grow in size compared with the rest  of our body increasing the brain to body  mass ratio or encephalization quotient  oh and DHA is also an antioxidant we'll  talk about antioxidants in a few minutes  but it seems like a pretty good  candidate for a brain food right so  what's the evidence that taking fish oil  will make you smarter or even stave off  decline let me read you the beginning of  a typical media piece published in 2012  on a major news site fish oils do make  children brainy runs the headline it  continues parents should give their  children a daily dose of fish oils if  they want to boost brainpower and stop  them being naughty Wow  where do I sign up going on scientists  at Oxford University found that taking a  Dayla daily omega-3 fatty acid  supplement improved not only the reading  ability but also behavior of primary  school children the study in the journal  PLoS ONE compared daily supplements of  omega-3 rich docos XM Noack acid DHA  with a placebo pretty compelling so  let's take a little closer look into the  study the design was a randomized  double-blind placebo-controlled study of  healthy seven to nine year old kids so  far so good they started out as  underperformers in reading okay that  leaves room for improvement right but it  also begs the question of what caused  them to have difficulty reading in the  first place the results no effect of DHA  on the full sample but in a subset of  the worst readers that  percentile and below the researchers did  find reading benefits and parents rated  their children is better behaved but  critically neither measures of working  memory nor teacher rated behavior were  different compared with the placebo  group so it's a pretty subtle finding  it's exciting to find that we can  improve reading ability in the kids who  most need help with a pretty simple  intervention but it's also possible that  they're having trouble reading in part  because they're malnourished and there's  no evidence that for kids who are  average readers supplementing their diet  with DHA makes any difference to  cognition or behavior so maybe omega-3  fatty acids aren't the brain boosters we  thought they were for all kids what  about for older adults we also often  hear about the power of omega-3s to  stave off cognitive effects of aging do  they well they do hold some promise  since older adults particularly those  who go on to develop Alzheimer's disease  tend to experience a decrease in levels  of DHA in their brains so scientists  have wondered whether this decrease  plays a role in the cognitive decline  that can accompany aging animals who are  deprived of DHA show problems with  learning which are then resolved if  they're given a supplement DHA  supplements are recommended by the  American Heart Association because they  seem to reduce the risk of coronary  artery disease and cardiovascular  problems contribute to cognitive decline  eating a lot of fish also seems to be  associated with a lower risk of stroke  they also reduce inflammation which has  been associated with neurodegenerative  diseases like Alzheimer's and omega-3s  might even play a direct role in  decreasing Alzheimer's pathology since  they reduce amyloid production and the  plaques that are a signature of disease  are made up of amyloid but a randomized  placebo-controlled double-blind study  published in Neurology in 2008 in which  302 healthy individuals aged 65 and  older participated the authors found no  affective DHA supplements on cognitive  function even after 26 weeks of taking  them and in a 2009 review of 11  observational and for clinical trials  but researchers at Johns Hopkins and  UCSF published in the nature club in the  nature clinical practice the authors  were surprised to find little evidence  of any positive effect in the prevention  or treatment of dementia they did report  some modest benefit in slowing decline  in healthy elderly participants so  perhaps for people at risk of developing  AD who are labeled as having mild  cognitive impairment there may be some  benefit as a randomized  placebo-controlled trial of patients  over the age of 55 published in 2010  found a little like the kids in the  lowest reading percentile older adults  who are vulnerable seem to show some  positive effects but people who are  healthy to begin with or those already  showing symptoms are not likely to find  themselves suddenly brainer so what can  we say about fish oil it likely won't  make a difference  unless you're pregnant under the age of  1 or at risk of showing cognitive  impairment either in childhood or in old  age I don't want to diminish the  potential benefits of staving off  decline but in this lecture we're  examining whether brain food actually  makes you smarter and there doesn't seem  to be any evidence of that  for DHA so much for omega-3 fatty acids  but what about other antioxidants we  often hear good things about green tea  coffee chocolate red wine blueberries  and strawberries all of which contain  antioxidants and are often labeled  superfoods is there any evidence that  they can boost our brains certainly lots  of companies that market specialty  drinks would like you to think so how  might antioxidants helped our cognitive  function well in a nutshell they're  involved in helping brain cells get  nutrients from the blood and in  neuroplasticity but they're best known  for defusing the little bombs that  result from normal chemical reactions  free radicals  oxygen is the stuff of life of course  but too much of it can be toxic no you  can't breathe yourself to death though  if you are getting oxygen from a  supplemental source when you're scuba  diving for example you can take in too  much oxygen and that can ultimately  cause problems like seizures we need  oxygen to extract energy from our food a  process that leaves behind metabolic  byproducts like free radicals these are  atoms that are electrically unstable we  have an unpaired electron and because of  this instability free radicals can strip  an electron from another nearby molecule  as they travel through the body they can  then wreak havoc by creating more  unstable molecules in their wake this  domino effect is how free radicals can  damage cells ripping through them like a  tornado and too many free radicals in a  person's body can become a problem  that's why the body has natural defenses  to protect against the proliferation of  these little bombs cells can keep free  radicals pinned in with physical  boundaries we have enzymes that  neutralized oxygen gone awry and of  course antioxidants from our diet can  donate their electrons to stop the chain  reaction that's all part of the normal  metabolic process but the question at  hand is whether more antioxidants will  enhance brain function or even protected  from decline studies and animal models  do show some positive effects of adding  antioxidants to the diet but the data  from human studies are mixed smaller  studies have reported some effects but  once those studies are replicated with  larger sample sizes the effects tend to  go away which means that there are  likely other variables affecting the  results in the smaller samples for  example one study in 2012 found a  positive effect on cognition in people  who ate more blueberries and  strawberries but people who have access  to these relatively expensive fruits are  better off and likely pay more attention  to their diets and lifestyle x-rays  more perhaps which we know affects  cognition and on the troubling side  studies have also shown that taking too  much of an antioxidant like vitamin E  for example can have negative side  effects like an increased risk of  prostate cancer there can be too much of  a good thing and taking vitamin  supplements is a good example of it  vitamins are a thirty billion or so  dollar business with surveys showing  that some fifty percent of Americans are  taking them and in 1988 an influential  placebo controlled study of twelve to  thirteen year olds showed an improvement  in nonverbal IQ for kids who took them  but since then the evidence is building  that adding vitamins to a healthy diet  doesn't help the brain even so research  does indicate that poor nutrition can  harm cognitive function so for kids who  are in danger of not getting enough  nutrients from their diet vitamins are a  good idea for everyone else it doesn't  seem to make a measurable difference so  we've talked about fish oil anti axe  oxidants and vitamins but what about  brain boosting drinks like those with  the prefix neuro in their names that we  talked about at the top of the lecture  you might have seen these bottles in  your local health food store marketing  better sleep sharper wits and less  stress some of these even contained  neurotransmitters and hormones but buyer  beware these drinks are classified of  dietary supplements not food or drugs so  they don't need FDA approval or even  safety testing to tout their benefits in  2016 neuro Brands LLC agreed to pay five  hundred thousand dollars in fines and  restitution in a legal settlement as  they were forced to change their  marketing this was in response to a  civil complaint alleging that there was  no scientific evidence to back up any of  their claims so there's not much  evidence that you can drink your way to  a better brain unless your drink is  laced with caffeine here we do have a  brain booster at our fingertips  some historians have even suggested that  the proliferation of coffee houses in  Europe in the middle of the 17th century  assured in the Enlightenment and  ultimately the Industrial Revolution  well when beer was the drink of choice  during the day coffee must have seemed  like the great cognitive enhancer and  coffee houses became places where  intellectuals gathered to exchange new  ideas but looking beyond its possible  impact on history what if science tell  us about caffeine and our brains well a  large study from 2002 found that higher  lifetime caffeine intake was associated  with better performance and older adults  on at least 6 of 12 tests of cognitive  function so notably this effect was only  found in women but if you're male don't  worry a later study from 2007 of  European men found that coffee drinkers  had a slower rate of cognitive decline  than those who abstained from caffeine  and in a study from 2009 moderate coffee  drinkers of both genders in midlife were  found to have a lower risk of developing  dementia and Alzheimer's disease later  in life can coffee stave off disease  well it's hard to say as of yet does  coffee leave a person more alert and  able to complete cognitive tasks and  thus score higher on such measures most  likely yes does it prevent plaques and  tangles which are the pathological  markers of Alzheimer's disease probably  not so caffeine might have a protective  effect but it remains unclear whether  it's a direct physiological mechanism  like reducing inflammation for example  or more related to increasing cognitive  resources like helping you focus better  on the task at hand so here again the  story is one of moderation because one  thing we do know that can lead to  increased Alzheimer's pathology is sleep  deprivation so as too much caffeine is  interfering with your sleep that's not  good and that brings us to our next  topic if we can't find a way to  boost our brains by adding things to our  diets what about taking things away here  my friends is a sad story one that I  tell a little reluctantly there does  seem to be some evidence that caloric  restriction eating substantially less  than most of us do may enhance cognition  and even extend your lifespan so I often  wonder whether the cost is worth it in  mouse models restricting calories  significantly by having them fast every  other day has been shown to improve  cognitive function and protect against  Alzheimer's Parkinson's Huntington's  other diseases and Stroke  what's more fasting while maintaining  essential vitamins and other nutrients  via supplements can induce the growth of  more new neurons in mice that's called  neurogenesis it can also positively  affect their neuroplasticity  the physical changes that cells undergo  during learning which might bend stave  off age-related cognitive decline and  restore function if the brain is injured  even just delaying the next meal seems  to enhance intelligence in mice even  when the ultimate calorie intake is the  same it seems that fasting can stimulate  the production of certain proteins and  hormones that improve function like B D  and s for example the star protein that  we talked about earlier evidence for the  cognitive benefits of caloric  restriction is plentiful among rodents  and there's some encouraging evidence  from studies involving humans and other  primates but the data here are mixed one  reason why we still don't know how  caloric restriction affects primates is  because our diets are so variable one  early study in monkeys for example found  a significant effect when the monkeys  were fed the equivalent of a junk food  diet in that case those who ate less  were better off but when monkeys were  put on a very healthy diet the effects  of restricting calories was mild or  non-existent we see the same in humans  of course not enough food is bad for  your brain too much unhealthy food is  also bad for your brain but we don't yet  know whether restricting calories in toe  can keep your brain young in fact one  global critique of the caloric  restriction literature is that even the  mouse studies are usually comparing  animals on a restricted diet with those  who can eat however much they want yeah  the restricted animals live longer than  those feeding ad libitum but already  affects due in part to the fact that the  unrestricted animals gain too much  weight the truth is is that we don't  know yet there is one pill so to speak  that does seem to reliably stave off  cognitive decline trigger the production  of BDNF and other helpful proteins and  boost your brain exercise in fact in now  classic experiments rodents that were  raised in an enriched environment with  more toys and other things in their  cages only showed a measurable increase  in BDNF and neurogenesis if they were  also provided with running wheels in  humans exercise also increases BDNF and  that might be the mechanism by which it  protects against neurodegenerative  diseases consistent aerobic exercise  increases the amount of grey matter in  your brain especially in the hippocampus  and prefrontal cortex which are  responsible for memory and cognitive  control if blueberries were as effective  we'd all be consuming them by the bushel  in fact walking has been shown to have  as big if not a bigger effect on  cognition in 70 and 80 year olds than  vitamins or other supplements so the  brain needs food and it needs exercise  but there's no compelling evidence yet  that some super foods are brain boosters  what about the other direction I can't  tell you how many times I've heard  parents attribute bad behavior in their  kids to sugar intake give a kid a  cupcake and she'll turn into a gremlin  right well maybe not as far back as 1995  a meta-analysis was designed to examine  the effects of sugar on the behavior and  cognition of children by then 23 studies  had already been conducted to address  the issue the final word no effect  when studies were carefully controlled  for expectation effects and measures  were objective consuming sugar had no  effect on most children and a study in  2006 replicated these results preschool  children were given either chocolate or  fruit and then videotaped these  videotapes were then watched by  objective raters who didn't know what  the children had consumed they found no  differences in behavior between kids  that ate chocolate and those who ate  fruit wait a minute you might be  thinking I've witnessed five-year-olds  at a birthday party their behavior is  monstrous but that's likely because  they're already overexcited the balloons  the scary clown the social interaction  so on it's not just the sugar and  remember that these are short-term  studies not studies of long-term  consumption of junk food long-term  studies do find negative effects of junk  food on cognition one study for example  tracked whether diet at age four  influences behavior at age seven and  here yes kids who had a diet high in  junk food eating chocolate bars and  chips four times a week were more  hyperactive and therefore less able to  concentrate and focus in class than kids  who had healthier diets at age seven  this has social implications of course  since kids of lower SES tend to consume  more junk food indeed the problem may  not be too much sugar but it might be  malnutrition in general since these same  kids are more likely to have litter  little or no omega-3 fatty acids for  instance in their diets  so maybe we can now put the brain food  myth largely to rest after several  decades of research we're not much  further along than what our grandparents  already knew eating a healthy diet and  getting a daily constitutional is good  for your brain excessive consumption of  pretty much anything is not but there's  another quiet revolution worth  considering pills that enhance your  brain these so called nootropics or  smart pills seem  to be more and more commonly used by  healthy people and the billion dollar  industry that supplies them is  blossoming most nootropics are  stimulants like caffeine that boost  cognition essentially by stating off  fatigue and thereby increasing mental  focus  these include drugs like adderall  ritalin and modafinil adderall and  Ritalin are traditionally prescribed for  the treatment of ADHD attention deficit  hyperactivity disorder by 2016  some 10% or more of kids aged 3 to 17  were being diagnosed and given  prescriptions of Ritalin or adderall  these kids grew up to the adults of  course and about 50% of them still  report symptoms so as these kids grow up  and move into careers many of them  continue taking these cognitive  enhancers even psychologists aren't  immune to the allure of the drugs in  fact in 2007 two prominent psychologists  at Cambridge University wrote a letter  to nature flagging the use of modafinil  by colleagues to stay competitive  adderall ritalin and modafinil are all  controlled substances in the US because  of their high potential for abuse they  all prevent brain cells from reabsorbing  the neurotransmitters norepinephrine and  dopamine which leaves more of them  available for use by the cells they  might seem harmless but they do have  side effects including sleep problems  anxiety headaches dizziness and an  increased heart rate among others we  don't allow our professional athletes to  take steroids and artificially boost  their muscle mass we do let them have a  cup of coffee or an energy drink so  various smart pills fall on this  continuum is it just a better cup of  coffee no you might argue it's a  controlled substance because it's  addictive well so is coffee as I'm very  well aware I don't have the answer to  the question of just how much regulation  should be applied to smart pills nor can  I predict how ubiquitous you might  become the next few decades will they  assure in the next revolution just as  coffee  supposedly did and enlightened Europe or  will some other brain booster take over  the industry  time will tell but there's one thing  that I can guarantee we won't stop  trying to make our brains better

